WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:02.100
Instructor: All right, so README.md,

00:02.100 --> 00:05.340
it's the name of the file, it's a Markdown file extension.

00:05.340 --> 00:06.173
In this video,

00:06.173 --> 00:08.970
we'll talk a little bit about Markdown itself, the syntax,

00:08.970 --> 00:11.220
and then in the next video, I'll make a README.

00:11.220 --> 00:13.350
So if you just wanna see making a README,

00:13.350 --> 00:15.510
go ahead and skip this if you already know Markdown.

00:15.510 --> 00:16.440
So as I mentioned,

00:16.440 --> 00:19.950
Markdown is this tool that generates markup.

00:19.950 --> 00:23.610
This is the official project documentation.

00:23.610 --> 00:26.580
It's pretty thin, it's very simple I guess.

00:26.580 --> 00:29.460
It says, "Markdown is text-to-HTML conversion tool

00:29.460 --> 00:31.020
for web writers.

00:31.020 --> 00:32.730
It allows you to write an easy-to-read,

00:32.730 --> 00:34.590
easy-to-write plain text format

00:34.590 --> 00:39.360
and then convert it to structurally valid XHTML or HTML."

00:39.360 --> 00:41.970
So for us, the benefit is that we can write

00:41.970 --> 00:44.400
this very succinct, easy to, hopefully,

00:44.400 --> 00:46.470
easy-to-write and remember syntax

00:46.470 --> 00:48.360
that will then turn into HTML,

00:48.360 --> 00:51.600
and, specifically, GitHub will turn it into HTML for us.

00:51.600 --> 00:54.870
If we take a look at, let's see,

00:54.870 --> 00:57.220
one of these READMEs, like this spaCy one here.

00:58.170 --> 01:01.260
Let's just go to the actual file, spaCy.

01:01.260 --> 01:05.490
Here's README, this is what GitHub shows me, right?

01:05.490 --> 01:07.230
There's a lot of stuff here.

01:07.230 --> 01:09.720
I mean all that text has to be written no matter what,

01:09.720 --> 01:11.730
but how would you indicate, you know,

01:11.730 --> 01:14.250
how would you make code blocks or a table,

01:14.250 --> 01:18.000
as you can see, like this table here, and all these links?

01:18.000 --> 01:19.920
If you know HTML, great.

01:19.920 --> 01:22.080
You can do that, but it still takes a lot of work.

01:22.080 --> 01:24.750
It's a lot of writing and additional syntax

01:24.750 --> 01:26.310
and HTML elements.

01:26.310 --> 01:29.220
So if we actually look at the raw Markdown,

01:29.220 --> 01:32.043
so this is the rendered, but this is the raw Markdown.

01:32.880 --> 01:34.680
It's a lot lighter weight.

01:34.680 --> 01:37.380
So it's still a lot of text 'cause it's a long README.

01:37.380 --> 01:39.750
But if you take a close look, we'll see things

01:39.750 --> 01:43.050
like hash symbols or octothorpes,

01:43.050 --> 01:44.940
stars or asterisks.

01:44.940 --> 01:48.180
This is how you make things bolded.

01:48.180 --> 01:50.100
Here's how you make a heading. Those ampers-

01:50.100 --> 01:52.170
Not ampersands, the hash signs again.

01:52.170 --> 01:55.530
Here's how you make a table. Here's how you make links.

01:55.530 --> 01:59.610
So this syntax is then read by GitHub

01:59.610 --> 02:02.460
and displayed as the output HTML

02:02.460 --> 02:04.650
that is generated by Markdown.

02:04.650 --> 02:06.900
So Markdown is not specific to GitHub.

02:06.900 --> 02:08.820
People use Markdown all over the place.

02:08.820 --> 02:11.340
There are tools that allow you to view Markdown

02:11.340 --> 02:13.020
and render it yourself.

02:13.020 --> 02:14.910
There's extensions for VS code.

02:14.910 --> 02:18.180
There are standalone applications that are just Markdown.

02:18.180 --> 02:20.700
And the one that I'll be using just in this video

02:20.700 --> 02:22.290
is this markdown-it demo.

02:22.290 --> 02:24.180
I'll include a link for it.

02:24.180 --> 02:27.810
So on the left, we can see some raw Markdown

02:27.810 --> 02:30.660
and on the right is the generated HTML.

02:30.660 --> 02:33.810
So I can just show some very quick examples.

02:33.810 --> 02:36.450
To make headings, as you can see here,

02:36.450 --> 02:37.920
there are six levels of headings

02:37.920 --> 02:40.890
just like in HTML if you're familiar with HTML.

02:40.890 --> 02:44.520
We have six elements for headings, h1 down to h6.

02:44.520 --> 02:46.890
So to make the main heading on a page

02:46.890 --> 02:48.840
of which there should usually just be one,

02:48.840 --> 02:51.690
we use a single hash sign and then a space

02:51.690 --> 02:53.340
and then some text.

02:53.340 --> 02:56.340
How about "I am a heading?"

02:56.340 --> 02:59.070
And then you can see our automatically generated output

02:59.070 --> 03:00.030
over here.

03:00.030 --> 03:03.123
So this is HTML that Markdown makes for us.

03:04.380 --> 03:06.810
This is the HTML, it made for me.

03:06.810 --> 03:09.540
So instead of having to write that or all of this,

03:09.540 --> 03:11.340
we can just write this.

03:11.340 --> 03:14.850
It's more compact and it's easier for most people to write,

03:14.850 --> 03:17.250
even if there's a bit of a learning curve at the beginning.

03:17.250 --> 03:18.870
You just have to remember, you know,

03:18.870 --> 03:20.820
what is the syntax to make a heading.

03:20.820 --> 03:22.500
All right, so one common pitfall

03:22.500 --> 03:24.180
is not putting spaces there.

03:24.180 --> 03:25.830
So that is no longer a heading.

03:25.830 --> 03:26.910
We need that space.

03:26.910 --> 03:30.360
And then if we have, you know, two, I won't show all six.

03:30.360 --> 03:34.803
But if we have two hash signs versus three,

03:35.790 --> 03:39.090
it's a smaller heading and three is even smaller.

03:39.090 --> 03:42.960
All the way down to six is the smallest that we can get.

03:42.960 --> 03:45.450
Okay, so that's how we can make headings.

03:45.450 --> 03:47.100
You can make horizontal rules.

03:47.100 --> 03:49.360
There's lines going across like this

03:49.360 --> 03:51.090
and I use that very often.

03:51.090 --> 03:54.240
And like this, I guess three asterisks.

03:54.240 --> 03:56.730
There are a whole bunch of other things you can do.

03:56.730 --> 03:59.790
You can make these different symbols somewhat easily.

03:59.790 --> 04:02.040
I never remember this though,

04:02.040 --> 04:05.970
but what I do use somewhat frequently is making text bolded.

04:05.970 --> 04:07.740
So using asterisks,

04:07.740 --> 04:12.300
I'll type something like "Hello there everyone!"

04:12.300 --> 04:13.830
And if I wanna bold something,

04:13.830 --> 04:16.740
like I'll bold the word "hello," I don't know why,

04:16.740 --> 04:19.300
I use two asterisks on either side

04:20.220 --> 04:21.870
and you can see it is bolded.

04:21.870 --> 04:26.870
If I instead do one star on something on either side,

04:27.150 --> 04:29.580
it becomes italicized.

04:29.580 --> 04:31.710
So bold is two stars.

04:31.710 --> 04:36.710
One star or asterisk around something makes it italics.

04:36.810 --> 04:38.850
You can do the same thing to make something strikethrough

04:38.850 --> 04:40.350
using two tildes.

04:40.350 --> 04:42.480
Don't really do that myself.

04:42.480 --> 04:46.230
Now, something I do pretty frequently is make blockquotes.

04:46.230 --> 04:49.860
So blockquotes start with a greater than sign

04:49.860 --> 04:52.020
and they look like this right here.

04:52.020 --> 04:53.253
So I'll show a demo.

04:54.240 --> 04:58.080
Let's see, I'm trying to make this as big as I can.

04:58.080 --> 05:00.900
Hopefully, this doesn't show up too small on the screen

05:00.900 --> 05:02.737
that you're watching this on.

05:02.737 --> 05:07.080
"I am a block quote." There we are.

05:07.080 --> 05:10.290
It turns into this block quote that's being rendered for me

05:10.290 --> 05:12.210
and I can have multiple lines.

05:12.210 --> 05:14.253
If I just wrap over to the next line,

05:15.210 --> 05:18.060
it still is considered part of that same line,

05:18.060 --> 05:19.350
the same block quote.

05:19.350 --> 05:22.740
So that is important when we're working with Markdown,

05:22.740 --> 05:26.380
it considers just a chunk of text like this

05:27.420 --> 05:28.800
to be one paragraph.

05:28.800 --> 05:32.700
And if I want spacing, I don't just hit Return like that,

05:32.700 --> 05:35.820
I have to have an actual empty line between them

05:35.820 --> 05:37.593
to generate two paragraphs.

05:38.520 --> 05:40.350
Additionally, we can make lists.

05:40.350 --> 05:42.510
So you can make an unordered list.

05:42.510 --> 05:45.780
Here, they're showing doing it with plus signs.

05:45.780 --> 05:49.593
Usually I actually make my list like this with an asterisk.

05:50.700 --> 05:55.020
So "first" and then another asterisk, "second."

05:55.020 --> 05:56.760
Right, that's how I can make a bulleted lists

05:56.760 --> 05:57.873
as you see there.

05:59.070 --> 06:02.130
So you can use a plus sign, a dash, or an asterisk

06:02.130 --> 06:02.963
to do that.

06:02.963 --> 06:06.900
And then you can make sublists by indenting two spaces.

06:06.900 --> 06:08.670
So I could do an example of that.

06:08.670 --> 06:13.650
We indent two spaces and then another star, "sublist."

06:13.650 --> 06:15.030
And you can see it is nested,

06:15.030 --> 06:18.090
it's further nested inside of that bullet.

06:18.090 --> 06:20.160
You can make ordered lists using numbers.

06:20.160 --> 06:22.410
I won't show you that, but you can see it here.

06:22.410 --> 06:25.830
And a really common important one is to make code blocks.

06:25.830 --> 06:28.050
So if you want a block of code,

06:28.050 --> 06:30.750
you can, first of all, just highlight a single line

06:30.750 --> 06:34.260
or a single phrase, or I guess a single line

06:34.260 --> 06:37.410
is what you would say, using these backtick characters.

06:37.410 --> 06:41.700
So we can put those backticks around like this word "code"

06:41.700 --> 06:43.770
and you can see it's rendered this way.

06:43.770 --> 06:47.430
So I can do that on "Indented" as an example.

06:47.430 --> 06:50.010
Those back ticks, that key is usually located

06:50.010 --> 06:52.263
above the Tab key on the left of a keyboard.

06:53.280 --> 06:57.960
And then you can also do blocks of code like this here

06:57.960 --> 07:01.230
or this block here by wrapping some code.

07:01.230 --> 07:02.790
So I'll just, I don't know,

07:02.790 --> 07:04.440
I'll type some random thing here,

07:05.370 --> 07:08.830
console.log("HELLO!")

07:10.170 --> 07:13.470
and then I'll duplicate that and just do two lines of code.

07:13.470 --> 07:14.880
So that's JavaScript.

07:14.880 --> 07:19.880
And if I wrap it with three backticks above and below,

07:20.400 --> 07:22.650
it turns into a block of code.

07:22.650 --> 07:25.230
And I can even add syntax highlighting

07:25.230 --> 07:27.240
if I use like, this is JavaScript,

07:27.240 --> 07:29.280
so if I specify js,

07:29.280 --> 07:32.700
now it's syntax highlighted as JavaScript.

07:32.700 --> 07:36.210
So this is just a very quick crash course in Markdown.

07:36.210 --> 07:38.100
It's not a huge topic, but you know,

07:38.100 --> 07:39.150
we could spend a lot more time

07:39.150 --> 07:41.880
talking about the nuances of syntax highlighting

07:41.880 --> 07:43.650
and what languages are supported.

07:43.650 --> 07:45.240
Here's how you make tables.

07:45.240 --> 07:49.110
Personally, I find it a pain to make tables in general.

07:49.110 --> 07:51.420
I don't use them that frequently in my READMEs.

07:51.420 --> 07:55.230
But anytime I do, I just copy from the docs.

07:55.230 --> 07:58.200
Now, links often trips students up.

07:58.200 --> 08:00.060
If you've ever worked on Reddit by the way,

08:00.060 --> 08:01.230
if you've ever written Reddit comments,

08:01.230 --> 08:03.960
I forgot to mention this, that they use Markdown,

08:03.960 --> 08:04.800
or at least they used to.

08:04.800 --> 08:05.910
I think they still do.

08:05.910 --> 08:09.330
So to make a link, you can write the text of a link

08:09.330 --> 08:13.230
inside of square brackets, like, whoops,

08:13.230 --> 08:16.177
trying to make this clear where I'm typing, like this,

08:16.177 --> 08:18.540
"Hello there click me!"

08:18.540 --> 08:22.980
And then immediately afterwards, we have parentheses

08:22.980 --> 08:26.433
and then some link like www.google.com.

08:27.270 --> 08:29.670
There we go. Now it has HTTP.

08:29.670 --> 08:31.500
And if I try and click on that,

08:31.500 --> 08:33.900
let's see, does it indeed take me to Google?

08:33.900 --> 08:34.743
Yes, it does.

08:35.760 --> 08:38.550
All right, so that's the basics of links.

08:38.550 --> 08:40.110
Then we have images.

08:40.110 --> 08:42.660
It's the same thing except we add an exclamation point

08:42.660 --> 08:43.530
in front.

08:43.530 --> 08:46.200
So this is the URL to to access that image.

08:46.200 --> 08:48.120
And then this would be the alternate text

08:48.120 --> 08:49.380
if that image doesn't load

08:49.380 --> 08:54.180
or if they're using a screen reader to read this alt text.

08:54.180 --> 08:56.280
So let's see, what else is there?

08:56.280 --> 09:00.300
There's some stuff around emojis, superscript, subscript,

09:00.300 --> 09:03.690
there's footnotes, there's definition lists, abbreviations,

09:03.690 --> 09:05.070
there's a lot more.

09:05.070 --> 09:07.650
And on GitHub, there's even some additional functionality,

09:07.650 --> 09:10.320
but that's just a quick crash course.

09:10.320 --> 09:13.290
We're not gonna spend a lot of time making fancy READMEs,

09:13.290 --> 09:14.700
but you obviously can.

09:14.700 --> 09:16.530
As you see here, I mean this is a table.

09:16.530 --> 09:17.670
It's got a bunch of emojis.

09:17.670 --> 09:21.930
In the table, there are links and they're bolded even.

09:21.930 --> 09:24.270
There's a lot of stuff going on in each one of these, right?

09:24.270 --> 09:27.480
We've got lists, headings, horizontal rules

09:27.480 --> 09:29.730
as you can see that line, well hopefully it shows up

09:29.730 --> 09:32.460
on the screencast, it's a little delicate.

09:32.460 --> 09:33.330
So there's a lot of stuff,

09:33.330 --> 09:37.380
code blocks, syntax highlighted code blocks.

09:37.380 --> 09:39.690
So we saw basically how we do this.

09:39.690 --> 09:42.210
We just would have to put all those pieces together.

09:42.210 --> 09:43.260
And in the next video,

09:43.260 --> 09:44.940
I'll show the process of making a README,

09:44.940 --> 09:47.240
pushing it up and writing some basic Markdown.
